1
00:00:01,168 --> 00:00:18,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, today's sutta is Samyutta Agama 616 and that is the Chinese parallel to the famous simile of the cook.

2
00:00:19,138 --> 00:00:20,438
[SPEAKER_00]: This one is kind of...

3
00:00:20,478 --> 00:00:22,760
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, maybe just because I'm used to that Pali sutta.

4
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:36,204
[SPEAKER_00]: This one seems more descriptive or something, I don't know, but maybe it's just a novelty, because it's written differently or something, but I still find it quite useful, so that's why I thought we can go through that.

5
00:00:37,784 --> 00:00:40,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, thus have I heard.

6
00:00:41,785 --> 00:00:47,887
[SPEAKER_00]: At one time, the Buddha was staying in Jeta Grove, Anathapindika's Park, in the country of Sravasti.

7
00:00:49,087 --> 00:00:51,668
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, the World Honored One said to the bhikkhus,

8
00:00:53,905 --> 00:00:56,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you should take the sign of your own citta.

9
00:00:57,008 --> 00:00:58,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Do not let it scatter outward.

10
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:01,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

11
00:01:02,732 --> 00:01:17,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if a bhikkhu is foolish, undiscerning and unskillful, not taking the sign of his own citta, but taking external signs instead, then afterward, he declines and gives rise to obstruction in himself.

12
00:01:20,605 --> 00:01:32,696
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a cook who is foolish, undiscerning and not skillful in the expedient means, mixing together the various flavors in service and of nourishment for his lord.

13
00:01:33,397 --> 00:01:35,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Sour, salty, pungent, bland.

14
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:40,663
[SPEAKER_00]: These do not accord with his lord's wishes, but he's unable

15
00:01:41,324 --> 00:01:43,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what does that remind you of?

16
00:02:05,532 --> 00:02:09,716
[SPEAKER_00]: If this doesn't accord with his lord's wishes, the lord is displeased.

17
00:02:09,756 --> 00:02:14,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Being displeased, he grants him neither rank nor reward, nor does he hold affection for him.

18
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:17,843
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's kind of guesswork, basically.

19
00:02:17,963 --> 00:02:26,270
[SPEAKER_00]: He just has his ideas, his preferences, that then he just kind of hopes would work out for the king.

20
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:28,581
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a hit and miss.

21
00:02:28,802 --> 00:02:32,025
[SPEAKER_00]: But even when it's a hit, he doesn't know why it is a hit.

22
00:02:32,766 --> 00:02:34,728
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it's a miss, he doesn't know why it's a miss.

23
00:02:35,529 --> 00:02:39,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, he's just locked in his own projections.

24
00:02:43,639 --> 00:02:46,262
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the foolish bhikkhu is the same.

25
00:02:47,569 --> 00:03:00,410
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, undiscerning and unskillful dwelling in the contemplation of the body within the body, he's unable to remove and cut off the higher afflictions, unable to gather and take hold of his own citta,

26
00:03:02,173 --> 00:03:16,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Likewise, unable to attain inward quietude of citta, unable to attain supreme right mindfulness, right clear knowing, likewise unable to attain the four kinds of higher citta states, the pleasant abiding and the secure nirvana not previously attained.

27
00:03:17,444 --> 00:03:18,864
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, he cannot

28
00:03:20,703 --> 00:03:36,617
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is called a bhikkhu who is foolish, undiscerning, unskillful, unable to skillfully gather the inner sign of citta while taking on external signs, thereby giving rise to obstructions in himself.

29
00:03:36,637 --> 00:03:45,886
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... we'll address, obviously, all these subtle points, but you can see the obstructions do not arise because you perceive things by sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

30
00:03:46,567 --> 00:03:52,633
[SPEAKER_00]: The obstructions arise because you are unable to skillfully gather the citta internally.

31
00:03:52,913 --> 00:03:57,478
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you are unable to maintain the context, contain the animal.

32
00:03:58,079 --> 00:03:59,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, these things obstruct you.

33
00:04:01,082 --> 00:04:02,703
[SPEAKER_00]: In and of themselves, they're not obstructions.

34
00:04:02,743 --> 00:04:05,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Obstructions come from undeveloped citta.

35
00:04:07,704 --> 00:04:22,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on the other hand, if there is a bhikkhu who is wise, intelligent, eloquent, skillful in expedient means, having first taken the inner sign of citta, afterward taking external signs, then at the later time he never declines, nor gives rise to obstruction himself.

36
00:04:24,475 --> 00:04:39,299
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a cook who is wise, intelligent, eloquent, and skillful in expedient means, serving and nourishing his lord, able to harmonize the various flavors, while grasping the sign of what his lord delights in, harmonizing the various flavors in accordance with his mind.

37
00:04:39,399 --> 00:04:51,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Listening to the flavors his lord desires, he repeatedly serves them up, and once the lord is pleased, he will certainly receive rank, emolument, and affection bestowed upon him grows even greater.

38
00:04:51,422 --> 00:04:53,163
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, he'll be rewarded for his efforts.

39
00:04:55,878 --> 00:04:57,078
[SPEAKER_00]: And same goes for the bhikkhu.

40
00:04:58,019 --> 00:05:17,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, correctly dwelling in contemplation of the body within the body, he cuts off the higher afflictions, skillfully gathers his citta, inwardly steals the citta, possesses right mindfulness, clear knowing, attains the four higher citta states, the pleasant abiding in this very life, attains the secure nirvana previously unattained.

41
00:05:19,170 --> 00:05:24,072
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is called a bhikkhu who is eloquent, skillful in expedient means, and so on.

42
00:05:25,545 --> 00:05:27,887
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes the same for the contemplation of feeling, mind and phenomena.

43
00:05:27,907 --> 00:05:29,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the four satipaṭṭhānas, obviously.

44
00:05:32,391 --> 00:05:35,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the bhikkhus rejoiced in the Buddha's words.

45
00:05:38,235 --> 00:05:48,704
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the main, like, the main point, really, main point from the sutta is just like the core, like in the Pali, is

46
00:05:51,887 --> 00:05:53,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the signs of your own citta.

47
00:05:54,189 --> 00:05:55,470
[SPEAKER_00]: We spoke about it many times.

48
00:05:56,972 --> 00:06:05,799
[SPEAKER_00]: The suttas mention it as a prerequisite to sotāpārthi, which also has its own prerequisite.

49
00:06:05,919 --> 00:06:14,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the sign of the mind was conditioned upon dwelling in seclusion, but not just some like a mechanical physical isolation,

50
00:06:15,873 --> 00:06:38,119
[SPEAKER_00]: But the actual, true viveka, internal viveka, internal seclusion, which is seclusion from lust, lustful intentions, let alone lustful acts, aversion, intentions of ill will, let alone, you know, acts of anger and so on, and even the delusion, obviously, the distraction and so on.

51
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:39,540
[SPEAKER_00]: So, internally,

52
00:06:40,572 --> 00:06:53,515
[SPEAKER_00]: means you would have had to be secluded from those things sufficiently enough, even if you're not necessarily able to abide in the physical seclusion, though, of course, physical seclusion help, can help, and always helps these things.

53
00:06:54,750 --> 00:06:58,192
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those are the prerequisites for seeing the signs of the mind.

54
00:06:58,332 --> 00:07:14,339
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, cittanimitta, as often heard and read about, can also become a thing that's sort of mystified as well, because people try to wrap their head around it, you know, what's cittanimitta, how to see my signs of the mind, which is a prerequisite for sotāpati and so on.

55
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:21,042
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then it's kind of, you know, gets equated with meditation experiences, visions and whatnot.

56
00:07:21,142 --> 00:07:21,343
[SPEAKER_00]: But,

57
00:07:23,233 --> 00:07:25,135
[SPEAKER_00]: That's really not what the suttas describe.

58
00:07:25,335 --> 00:07:27,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither this āgama parallel, nor the actual Pali.

59
00:07:28,018 --> 00:07:33,183
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really just something that you can see, if you know where to look.

60
00:07:33,304 --> 00:07:40,691
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like hidden behind a veil of ignorance, that you just need to, through some, you know, magical absorption, kind of, wake up to.

61
00:07:41,564 --> 00:07:47,066
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically, if the mind is obstructed, as the Sutta says, it's because of your doing.

62
00:07:47,566 --> 00:08:02,090
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you're foolish, you're incompetent, you don't know what to pay attention to, you're ignoring what... how King reacts and what he does, and just serve your own preferences on top of it, and are absolutely convinced and locked in those ideas.

63
00:08:02,590 --> 00:08:06,671
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you don't see your own mind.

64
00:08:06,791 --> 00:08:09,412
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like the first question,

65
00:08:12,678 --> 00:08:20,844
[SPEAKER_00]: that I posted was pretty much like... how to see?

66
00:08:22,345 --> 00:08:24,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, do I see signs of my mind?

67
00:08:24,506 --> 00:08:27,468
[SPEAKER_00]: You can ask yourself that question.

68
00:08:27,868 --> 00:08:29,990
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the answer is no, why not?

69
00:08:31,191 --> 00:08:39,196
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, there is no an external condition that's preventing you from seeing the internal signs of your citta, as the sutra says.

70
00:08:41,258 --> 00:08:43,919
[SPEAKER_00]: External signs, external conditions remain unchanged.

71
00:08:44,460 --> 00:08:47,501
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they play no part in you not seeing the signs of the citta.

72
00:08:47,541 --> 00:08:48,041
[SPEAKER_00]: What does?

73
00:08:49,042 --> 00:08:50,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there has to be a reason for obstruction.

74
00:08:51,423 --> 00:08:58,827
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't be, oh, I'm doing everything right, but I'm not still seeing the signs of my mind.

75
00:08:59,747 --> 00:08:59,867
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

76
00:09:00,428 --> 00:09:04,350
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're doing everything right, there would be no obstructions that are obstructing your own mind.

77
00:09:04,410 --> 00:09:06,311
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would be seeing the signs of it.

78
00:09:06,951 --> 00:09:09,232
[SPEAKER_00]: The indications, the characteristics, and so on.

79
00:09:11,097 --> 00:09:15,341
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ask yourself, do I see the signs of my mind?

80
00:09:15,521 --> 00:09:16,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And the answer is no.

81
00:09:16,662 --> 00:09:17,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Why not?

82
00:09:18,243 --> 00:09:21,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, again, let that question sink in.

83
00:09:21,967 --> 00:09:29,473
[SPEAKER_00]: What is now stopping me from seeing the internal characteristics of my own wild animal?

84
00:09:31,855 --> 00:09:35,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what is the... what is the convergence point?

85
00:09:37,078 --> 00:09:49,364
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a, say, like you could say, you know, seclusion necessary, withdrawal from unwholesome states, withdrawal from sensuality, withdrawal from ill-will, withdrawal from distraction, withdrawal from hindrances.

86
00:09:49,384 --> 00:09:51,425
[SPEAKER_00]: What do all these things have in common?

87
00:09:54,106 --> 00:10:02,851
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense, what do... What is the main carrier, let's put it that way, of all those things, of all these obstructive states?

88
00:10:04,087 --> 00:10:12,790
[SPEAKER_00]: What defines obstruction as obstruction and non-obstruction as non-obstruction?

89
00:10:13,131 --> 00:10:18,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much, what is a single factor because of which you're not seeing the signs of your mind yet?

90
00:10:24,455 --> 00:10:27,856
[SPEAKER_00]: And the answer is... it's your intention.

91
00:10:28,717 --> 00:10:29,297
[SPEAKER_00]: It's cetanā.

92
00:10:30,908 --> 00:10:37,630
[SPEAKER_00]: And, even linguistically speaking, citta and cetanā carries that connection.

93
00:10:37,730 --> 00:10:47,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Citta as a sort of a, say, you could say, accumulated horizon, and cetanā would be the expression of it.

94
00:10:49,233 --> 00:10:49,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

95
00:10:52,448 --> 00:11:02,838
[SPEAKER_00]: You start by your own intentions being driven, by ignorance, by hindrances, by aversion, by greed, by lust, by acts out, by body, by speech, by mind.

96
00:11:03,858 --> 00:11:09,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you come across the dhamma, you learn it rightly, you start to actually train in virtue and so on.

97
00:11:10,717 --> 00:11:25,041
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like all of that, the sole purpose of that, is to, well, first reduce, then contain and prevent the expressions of corrupted citta, of the wild animal, as I often compare it.

98
00:11:28,822 --> 00:11:38,705
[SPEAKER_00]: But that in and of itself, like abstaining from certain behaviors, as necessary as it is, in and of itself, will not necessarily

99
00:11:40,193 --> 00:11:45,215
[SPEAKER_00]: be able to address the mental aspect of those intentions.

100
00:11:46,336 --> 00:11:52,278
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what's missing for people that, say, are keeping the precepts of training rightly, but still not being sotāpānas.

101
00:11:53,759 --> 00:12:05,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Because on the level of these, like, mental layers, the unwholesome intentions are still not sufficiently seen, sufficiently restrained.

102
00:12:06,344 --> 00:12:08,385
[SPEAKER_00]: They are still entertained and accepted.

103
00:12:12,135 --> 00:12:13,116
[SPEAKER_00]: At least mentally.

104
00:12:13,257 --> 00:12:14,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe you don't say anything bad.

105
00:12:15,259 --> 00:12:17,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe you don't do anything by the body.

106
00:12:18,143 --> 00:12:22,069
[SPEAKER_00]: But mentally, when those intentions present themselves, you do take them up.

107
00:12:23,090 --> 00:12:23,911
[SPEAKER_00]: You do accept them.

108
00:12:24,152 --> 00:12:27,557
[SPEAKER_00]: You do allow them to occupy you, to proliferate.

109
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:43,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the single factor for somebody not being a sotāpanna is not having fully, sufficiently contained, endured rightly and eventually uprooted or seen beyond the roots of the unwholesome intentions.

110
00:12:45,468 --> 00:12:47,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And consequently, that's how you would do it.

111
00:12:50,051 --> 00:12:52,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to

112
00:12:53,455 --> 00:12:59,158
[SPEAKER_00]: See the signs of the mind, so that you can, you know, understand the nature of dukkha and Four Noble Truths and be a Sotāpanna.

113
00:12:59,678 --> 00:13:12,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the entire training, everything that you've been doing so far and you're planning to do, pretty much needs to be leading up to the point of recognizing the unwholesome intentions, even on the mental level.

114
00:13:15,278 --> 00:13:19,240
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that's the whole, you know, so many talks, in so many other talks that I often talk about.

115
00:13:19,921 --> 00:13:35,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you know, like you can, as I said, precepts are necessary, virtue is necessary, sense restraint is necessary, but you can adhere to it, you can take it on and kind of get used to it and really never break the precepts for a hundred years.

116
00:13:36,091 --> 00:13:44,656
[SPEAKER_00]: But at the same time, never allow those same principles of precepts and sense restraint to come too close on those mental levels.

117
00:13:45,707 --> 00:13:46,888
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a bit much.

118
00:13:47,248 --> 00:13:53,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you tolerate it there, you allow it there, you engage with it there, but you never let it go too far.

119
00:13:54,214 --> 00:13:55,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's not good enough.

120
00:13:55,455 --> 00:13:57,516
[SPEAKER_00]: If your goal is right view, that's not good enough.

121
00:13:58,437 --> 00:14:08,645
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the measure of suitable intentions, skillful intentions, or unskillful that obstruct, and skillful that don't obstruct,

122
00:14:10,880 --> 00:14:13,141
[SPEAKER_00]: is by your own presently enduring intention.

123
00:14:13,221 --> 00:14:17,182
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not by your views or ideas or how you think things should be.

124
00:14:17,242 --> 00:14:26,245
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's kind of that second part where it says the foolish cook has his own ideas what to do, but he has no reference point

125
00:14:27,857 --> 00:14:29,897
[SPEAKER_00]: And most people's Buddhist practice is that today.

126
00:14:30,537 --> 00:14:43,900
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, mechanical techniques, absorption methods, I don't know, squint three feet in front of your eye for three minutes before you... like these random things, random observances, random exercises, body scanning and stuff.

127
00:14:45,380 --> 00:14:53,841
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that relate to the actual, you know, citta, the king, in terms of which direction is it sending the animal?

128
00:14:53,962 --> 00:14:55,242
[SPEAKER_00]: What message is it conveying?

129
00:14:55,282 --> 00:14:56,242
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no idea.

130
00:14:57,038 --> 00:14:58,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't even see the king.

131
00:14:59,119 --> 00:15:03,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, king's mind, king's preferences do not exist for a foolish person.

132
00:15:04,122 --> 00:15:15,209
[SPEAKER_00]: He's been told he has to serve the meal and he serves it according to his own concocted ideas, theory, you know, tradition, authority, whatever he chose to use to justify.

133
00:15:15,829 --> 00:15:18,811
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that results in he himself not seeing his own intentions.

134
00:15:19,930 --> 00:15:32,476
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why he's never gonna see the king and never be able to actually address it right and recognize, oh, hold on, when I attend to these things, when I intend them, act out, this is the result of my citta.

135
00:15:34,317 --> 00:15:35,677
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, it doesn't matter why.

136
00:15:36,598 --> 00:15:41,580
[SPEAKER_00]: You could say, well, the whole, the entire world does this thing and it's not a problem.

137
00:15:42,340 --> 00:15:43,742
[SPEAKER_00]: And that might be objectively true.

138
00:15:44,403 --> 00:15:48,307
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not true for you in terms of your practice and where the values should be.

139
00:15:49,088 --> 00:15:59,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, for whatever reason, when I do that thing that the whole world does, my mind gets enraged, or my mind gets infected with lust, or my mind gets distracted.

140
00:16:00,717 --> 00:16:03,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I can say, yeah, but, you know, that's secondary.

141
00:16:03,101 --> 00:16:07,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Really, it's not a problem, because if it's a problem, the world wouldn't be doing it.

142
00:16:07,207 --> 00:16:07,367
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

143
00:16:07,548 --> 00:16:08,710
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the wrong reference.

144
00:16:08,770 --> 00:16:12,515
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that projected idea of a foolish man who thinks, I'll be doing this.

145
00:16:12,696 --> 00:16:15,921
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't really matter what my own mind, what the king is hinting at.

146
00:16:17,873 --> 00:16:32,703
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where you need to... the beginning of, like, like, discernment of that reference point of your own wild animal, of your own citta, is... you gotta start, first and foremost, recognizing the pretty much the broader sense that contains... of your own intentions.

147
00:16:34,345 --> 00:16:36,726
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole world is doing this.

148
00:16:37,047 --> 00:16:37,787
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a problem.

149
00:16:37,927 --> 00:16:40,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but why am I doing that?

150
00:16:41,810 --> 00:16:44,972
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the... that's the question you always need to have at the back of your mind.

151
00:16:45,032 --> 00:16:47,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much as an enduring context.

152
00:16:48,690 --> 00:16:53,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever you're doing, you're looking as to, why am I doing this?

153
00:16:53,595 --> 00:17:04,125
[SPEAKER_00]: And, initially, as I said, maybe for somebody new to this, it can be a bit kind of, oh, I'm doing this because of this or because of that, and you fall into this psychological kind of patterns and stuff.

154
00:17:04,686 --> 00:17:08,810
[SPEAKER_00]: You calm down, but the context will remain of whatever's done.

155
00:17:09,070 --> 00:17:12,854
[SPEAKER_00]: At the same time, you're looking as to, what's my reason for doing it?

156
00:17:13,651 --> 00:17:25,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, not to arrive at some great revelation, or I'm doing it because of this, or justification for that matter, but to see whether it's rooted in lust, aversion, distraction, i.e.

157
00:17:25,235 --> 00:17:29,896
[SPEAKER_00]: delusion, or other hindrances, if you want to approach it through a hindrance side.

158
00:17:30,616 --> 00:17:31,076
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why.

159
00:17:31,797 --> 00:17:32,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's your reference.

160
00:17:33,715 --> 00:17:39,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you would become truly experienced, the true viveka, from unwholesome states.

161
00:17:40,060 --> 00:17:45,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by going on top of a mountain, which, as I said, can help, but in and of itself, it's not going to do the work for you.

162
00:17:47,346 --> 00:18:02,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only way to truly seclude yourself, as a prerequisite to see the signs of your mind, the only way to truly seclude yourself from unwholesome states, is through discerning your intentions and not fueling the unwholesome ones, the obstructive ones.

163
00:18:02,903 --> 00:18:06,885
[SPEAKER_00]: You do that long enough, you'll be secluded from all the obstruction.

164
00:18:07,206 --> 00:18:13,189
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're secluded from all the obstructions, the prerequisite for seeing the signs of the mind, dwelling in seclusion, will be met.

165
00:18:14,610 --> 00:18:27,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you start seeing your own mind as an independent living entity, pretty much in the heart of your being, not a theoretical projection, not a mystical belief or some, you know, magical hope.

166
00:18:27,998 --> 00:18:28,178
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

167
00:18:28,298 --> 00:18:31,080
[SPEAKER_00]: The actual living thing.

168
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:31,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Right there.

169
00:18:32,758 --> 00:18:33,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Like mind within the mind.

170
00:18:34,119 --> 00:18:34,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it that way.

171
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:37,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Like now, whenever you think my mind is just my mind.

172
00:18:37,881 --> 00:18:38,522
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you see.

173
00:18:39,302 --> 00:18:45,346
[SPEAKER_00]: But you discern that your own mind, whatever idea of my mind I have as my mind, has its own mind.

174
00:18:46,827 --> 00:18:48,429
[SPEAKER_00]: And I have no access to that mind.

175
00:18:51,611 --> 00:18:53,152
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't decide for it.

176
00:18:53,732 --> 00:19:00,977
[SPEAKER_00]: I just meet it, meet it, whichever way basically has changed or a reason for me.

177
00:19:02,421 --> 00:19:06,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly what Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta describes.

178
00:19:06,104 --> 00:19:08,045
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows mind of lust as mind of lust.

179
00:19:08,105 --> 00:19:11,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Not he changes mind of lust into something more suitable.

180
00:19:11,388 --> 00:19:11,508
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

181
00:19:11,548 --> 00:19:16,892
[SPEAKER_00]: By knowing it for what it is, you see where the line of your effort and your responsibility is.

182
00:19:17,797 --> 00:19:32,584
[SPEAKER_00]: By not knowing what it is, where it is, to what extent it is, by not seeing that king actually has preferences to what you're doing, you just be stabbing in the dark, as most people do, and just do this and do that, guided by your own preference of what feels good for you.

183
00:19:33,364 --> 00:19:33,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for the king.

184
00:19:34,265 --> 00:19:34,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for the mind.

185
00:19:35,445 --> 00:19:36,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for the citta.

186
00:19:36,166 --> 00:19:37,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for the mind within the mind.

187
00:19:39,387 --> 00:19:40,768
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're secluded enough,

188
00:19:42,087 --> 00:19:47,754
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll see the signs of that mind within the mind, an independent mind of your mind, sufficiently enough.

189
00:19:48,435 --> 00:19:52,940
[SPEAKER_00]: You see that, you do have a prerequisite for understanding the nature of dukkha, because that's the root of suffering.

190
00:19:52,960 --> 00:19:55,984
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the root of craving, which is the root of suffering.

191
00:19:56,004 --> 00:19:56,364
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind.

192
00:19:56,485 --> 00:19:57,526
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is the root of craving.

193
00:19:57,546 --> 00:19:58,427
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind that you're not seeing.

194
00:19:59,802 --> 00:20:22,739
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, pretty much, somebody who is seeing their mind on account of sufficiently maintaining, protecting the context of beneficial intentions and not fueling the unbeneficial ones, regardless of what the world, authority, tradition, peers, whoever says, your intention

195
00:20:23,775 --> 00:20:31,391
[SPEAKER_00]: The roots of your intention are the criteria for whether you will be tolerating it mentally, acting out of it physically or not.

196
00:20:33,416 --> 00:20:33,937
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

197
00:20:35,071 --> 00:20:43,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's also not incidental, why nobody can just choose to do that kind of spontaneously.

198
00:20:43,037 --> 00:20:57,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'll just spontaneously ignore the pressure of the senses, the pressure of the world, the pressure of the authority, the pressure of the history, of the tradition, and just stay on this level of like, yeah, but my intention is rooted in lust, therefore I won't follow it.

199
00:20:59,028 --> 00:21:01,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you really need to have a good reason to stick there.

200
00:21:02,665 --> 00:21:06,987
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, again, the learning and the right instruction is obviously very important.

201
00:21:07,167 --> 00:21:08,127
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't start rightly.

202
00:21:08,207 --> 00:21:10,488
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you can have all the instruction you want.

203
00:21:10,548 --> 00:21:16,611
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have the Buddha himself reciting dhamma for you 50 years.

204
00:21:17,031 --> 00:21:25,374
[SPEAKER_00]: If, again, you are not looking at the intentions behind your actions, behind your speech, behind your thoughts, you're not going to do the work.

205
00:21:27,755 --> 00:21:29,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do I see the signs of my mind?

206
00:21:29,936 --> 00:21:30,176
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

207
00:21:30,697 --> 00:21:31,398
[SPEAKER_00]: No, maybe.

208
00:21:31,638 --> 00:21:33,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, why not?

209
00:21:35,001 --> 00:21:35,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Why not?

210
00:21:36,343 --> 00:21:38,004
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the unwholesome?

211
00:21:38,445 --> 00:21:39,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Unwholesome as in obstructive.

212
00:21:39,606 --> 00:21:40,206
[SPEAKER_00]: They might not be...

213
00:21:40,527 --> 00:21:45,311
[SPEAKER_00]: These intentions might not be overtly, kind of coarsely, bad.

214
00:21:45,952 --> 00:21:52,398
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, deep down, you know, whenever you're about to do something, say something, you know where it is rooted.

215
00:21:54,195 --> 00:21:57,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's, you know, subtle desire, subtle restlessness, subtle this, subtle that.

216
00:21:57,776 --> 00:22:01,538
[SPEAKER_00]: And that needs to be seen sufficiently enough and recognized and familiarized with.

217
00:22:01,918 --> 00:22:05,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is pretty much the gateway to discerning the animal.

218
00:22:07,119 --> 00:22:10,701
[SPEAKER_00]: That then needs taming and training.

219
00:22:12,401 --> 00:22:21,945
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that was kind of the second question that came on the base of this sutta was like the foolish cook serves according to his own ideas.

220
00:22:21,965 --> 00:22:22,945
[SPEAKER_00]: So, same, as I said.

221
00:22:24,682 --> 00:22:30,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, you know, I like to sit and do the body scanning every night.

222
00:22:31,466 --> 00:22:31,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

223
00:22:33,547 --> 00:22:34,127
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the reason?

224
00:22:34,167 --> 00:22:35,028
[SPEAKER_00]: For you specifically.

225
00:22:37,409 --> 00:22:41,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever practice you're doing, why?

226
00:22:44,492 --> 00:22:46,293
[SPEAKER_00]: What's your reason?

227
00:22:46,533 --> 00:22:47,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see any connection?

228
00:22:49,070 --> 00:22:55,273
[SPEAKER_00]: To the citta, to the training, do you see how it how it translates, how it relates, how it conveys the messages?

229
00:22:56,954 --> 00:23:09,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there such a such a kind of like a like a field that's that's that's connected to that, you know, the the citta and the inner signs of training?

230
00:23:10,239 --> 00:23:11,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it like...

231
00:23:11,960 --> 00:23:15,782
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't really know, because I'm told to, because I've been trying and I found it helpful,

232
00:23:18,235 --> 00:23:19,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Helpful in what way?

233
00:23:21,176 --> 00:23:21,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Pacifying you?

234
00:23:24,557 --> 00:23:25,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Removing the disturbances?

235
00:23:27,738 --> 00:23:29,178
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, many things can do that.

236
00:23:29,619 --> 00:23:33,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that make them unobstructive, in terms of seeing the dhamma?

237
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:37,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what's the criteria that you follow when you practice?

238
00:23:39,141 --> 00:23:44,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what's the criteria that the foolish cook... By the way, you're a foolish cook, if you're not seeing your mind.

239
00:23:45,683 --> 00:23:47,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody starts as a foolish cook.

240
00:23:48,050 --> 00:23:48,570
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

241
00:23:49,211 --> 00:23:51,712
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, I did something wrong and I became a foolish cook.

242
00:23:51,732 --> 00:23:51,852
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

243
00:23:51,932 --> 00:23:53,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody starts as a foolish cook.

244
00:23:53,353 --> 00:23:54,494
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't change that.

245
00:23:56,855 --> 00:23:59,617
[SPEAKER_00]: But, instead of ignoring it, yeah, you accept it.

246
00:23:59,677 --> 00:24:00,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, hold on.

247
00:24:01,037 --> 00:24:07,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the Sutta clearly says, if I'm not seeing my own mind, it's because I am maintaining the obstructions of it.

248
00:24:07,681 --> 00:24:09,742
[SPEAKER_00]: I am foolish in that regard.

249
00:24:09,822 --> 00:24:14,405
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean like, oh, I'm an idiot, worthless person or something.

250
00:24:14,425 --> 00:24:14,765
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

251
00:24:14,785 --> 00:24:15,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are two coarse things.

252
00:24:15,946 --> 00:24:17,247
[SPEAKER_00]: This is like on the level of

253
00:24:18,203 --> 00:24:18,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Hold on.

254
00:24:19,504 --> 00:24:20,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I seeing my intentions?

255
00:24:23,567 --> 00:24:32,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I using the criteria of those intentions for whether I should follow inclinations to say something, do something, think something, or not?

256
00:24:33,095 --> 00:24:34,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I just ignoring that?

257
00:24:35,337 --> 00:24:43,263
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everybody starts as a foolish cook, but nobody needs to stay a foolish cook, and people won't stay a foolish cook if they make the effort.

258
00:24:45,958 --> 00:24:51,140
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever practice you're doing, you need to know why you're doing it.

259
00:24:53,441 --> 00:25:02,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there is no external justification, external signs that can justify ignoring the internal context, the internal counterpart.

260
00:25:04,304 --> 00:25:05,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing can come in place of that.

261
00:25:08,746 --> 00:25:14,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever practice you're doing, sit down and ask yourself, why am I doing it?

262
00:25:14,909 --> 00:25:15,730
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the benefit?

263
00:25:16,430 --> 00:25:24,116
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, whatever answer comes up, question that.

264
00:25:24,136 --> 00:25:33,064
[SPEAKER_00]: And same, even if, say, if you're not doing the mindless techniques or body scanning and stuff like that, that I always criticize, say, yeah, I'm keeping the precepts and reading the suttas.

265
00:25:33,764 --> 00:25:34,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

266
00:25:34,905 --> 00:25:38,768
[SPEAKER_00]: When you are actually doing those things, when you're making the effort to sustain those things, why?

267
00:25:41,631 --> 00:25:44,313
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why you need to ask those questions, because

268
00:25:45,447 --> 00:25:56,373
[SPEAKER_00]: There's gonna be like a... already a lot of implicit expectations that will remain uncovered.

269
00:25:56,433 --> 00:26:02,456
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, I'm listening to Ajahn Amolie and, you know, I'm not doing, as I said, mindless techniques or something.

270
00:26:02,476 --> 00:26:03,516
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm keeping the precepts and stuff.

271
00:26:03,996 --> 00:26:04,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

272
00:26:05,217 --> 00:26:05,537
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

273
00:26:05,557 --> 00:26:08,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Because deep down, I expect something will happen.

274
00:26:08,719 --> 00:26:11,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Something right will happen because I found the best teacher now.

275
00:26:12,281 --> 00:26:14,882
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, as I said, you can find the Buddha himself.

276
00:26:16,590 --> 00:26:18,512
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't result in what you're hoping to result.

277
00:26:18,572 --> 00:26:18,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

278
00:26:18,893 --> 00:26:36,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're maintaining that gap between things actually done, in terms of practice, by body, speech and mind, such as not cultivating, as I said, the bad bodily behavior, verbal behavior, but then training in the same principles of the mental behavior,

279
00:26:40,617 --> 00:27:02,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person wouldn't go as far and then would adopt the practice through the external signs, through the talks and suttas and stuff, but leave that internal gap where the intentions are, filled with hope and expectation that these external things I'm doing will bring up citta, nimitta and sotāpati.

280
00:27:03,854 --> 00:27:13,802
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is not as wrong as say somebody completely mindlessly just doing mechanical body scanning without a grain of thought or self-reflection, but it's still on the same level.

281
00:27:15,223 --> 00:27:18,606
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still not gonna result in the right view.

282
00:27:19,987 --> 00:27:32,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the intentions, your own intentions, your own cetanā, which is always manifested from that citta, which is a horizon for it, so to speak, remains completely ignored.

283
00:27:33,548 --> 00:27:35,229
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like invisible or not seen.

284
00:27:35,549 --> 00:27:41,031
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just not valued enough to be used sufficiently as a reference point.

285
00:27:41,131 --> 00:27:41,832
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the difference.

286
00:27:41,912 --> 00:27:44,913
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, my intentions are hidden from me or something.

287
00:27:44,953 --> 00:27:56,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the most inauthentic person that just lives perpetually in a state of bad faith, consumed by sensuality, ill will, and just acting, reacting to impulses, even they

288
00:27:57,966 --> 00:28:01,828
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot truly obscure their intention at any given time.

289
00:28:01,928 --> 00:28:07,111
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why deep down everybody knows what they're doing and why they're doing it.

290
00:28:07,491 --> 00:28:15,375
[SPEAKER_00]: They might ignore it, they might keep the momentum going so that they never had to stop and look back at it, but that cannot be covered.

291
00:28:16,276 --> 00:28:17,577
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just the nature of the mind.

292
00:28:17,617 --> 00:28:19,678
[SPEAKER_00]: You are paired with the citta, you're paired with the animal.

293
00:28:19,998 --> 00:28:25,361
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't... you can be covering it up all you want, it's always going to be there right underneath.

294
00:28:26,471 --> 00:28:27,171
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the point.

295
00:28:28,272 --> 00:28:31,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Cetanas, your intentions, are not mysteriously hidden.

296
00:28:31,553 --> 00:28:35,214
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to do a new set of practice to reveal that.

297
00:28:35,234 --> 00:28:35,354
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

298
00:28:35,414 --> 00:28:35,894
[SPEAKER_00]: They're there.

299
00:28:36,154 --> 00:28:36,734
[SPEAKER_00]: They're seen.

300
00:28:37,075 --> 00:28:37,955
[SPEAKER_00]: They're engaged with.

301
00:28:38,495 --> 00:28:41,096
[SPEAKER_00]: They're multiplied through your own reflection and chosen and stuff.

302
00:28:41,116 --> 00:28:44,137
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just not valued enough as something to stick to.

303
00:28:45,797 --> 00:28:47,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have... it's not a big thing.

304
00:28:48,138 --> 00:28:48,818
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a big problem.

305
00:28:48,858 --> 00:28:49,799
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a thought, you know.

306
00:28:51,099 --> 00:28:52,380
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm not gonna say anything.

307
00:28:52,500 --> 00:28:53,380
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not gonna do anything.

308
00:28:54,655 --> 00:28:56,817
[SPEAKER_00]: But, when it comes up, that choice...

309
00:28:56,857 --> 00:28:57,938
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always a choice, by the way.

310
00:28:59,559 --> 00:29:05,303
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how the Nyanamoli Thera, the first, and Nyanavira used to translate it, if I remember correctly, in their early letters.

311
00:29:05,924 --> 00:29:09,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they're talking about translating cetanā as choice.

312
00:29:10,147 --> 00:29:12,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's always on the level when you exercise choice.

313
00:29:12,569 --> 00:29:16,772
[SPEAKER_00]: And you do exercise choice, which means there is always self-awareness.

314
00:29:17,052 --> 00:29:20,515
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot exercise a choice when you are unaware.

315
00:29:22,074 --> 00:29:25,696
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can do a choice habitually, but there is still awareness of the choice.

316
00:29:25,736 --> 00:29:28,058
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just ignoring it and not emphasizing it enough.

317
00:29:28,918 --> 00:29:37,624
[SPEAKER_00]: And I've been talking about it in previous talks, like, recognizing that choice and sticking to the right one whenever there is enough self-awareness to know that, oh, that's that choice.

318
00:29:38,724 --> 00:29:40,205
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not going to be perfect from the start.

319
00:29:40,245 --> 00:29:43,787
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going to be, you know, ignoring, falling for it, distracted from it and so on.

320
00:29:44,528 --> 00:29:50,812
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you learn enough, you'll be catching it more and more, the moments when you make this choice, choices,

321
00:29:51,619 --> 00:29:54,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Before you make them and then not make them, that's the practice.

322
00:29:55,100 --> 00:30:01,103
[SPEAKER_00]: The practice is not, oh, I'm aware of the moment now and I feel my body and the universe and experience as a whole or whatever.

323
00:30:01,123 --> 00:30:01,683
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no, no, no.

324
00:30:02,323 --> 00:30:04,544
[SPEAKER_00]: The practice is not to make the wrong choice.

325
00:30:07,806 --> 00:30:08,726
[SPEAKER_00]: And what's the wrong choice?

326
00:30:08,786 --> 00:30:10,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, anything that obstructs.

327
00:30:10,327 --> 00:30:11,527
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it that obstructs?

328
00:30:11,567 --> 00:30:17,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it obstructs the mind if it's rooted in lust, desire, greed,

329
00:30:18,915 --> 00:30:33,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Aversion, ill will, irritation, distraction, mindlessness, laziness, spread it to the hindrances, in all sorts of synonyms and whatever works for you.

330
00:30:34,802 --> 00:30:36,202
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the point.

331
00:30:36,262 --> 00:30:43,325
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but, also, at the same time, if it's not rooted in that, you don't need to... you don't need... you can choose it.

332
00:30:44,206 --> 00:30:44,926
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no problem.

333
00:30:45,760 --> 00:30:48,381
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, it can feel like, oh, I have to police everything.

334
00:30:48,421 --> 00:30:50,583
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just so confining, so restricting.

335
00:30:51,403 --> 00:30:56,426
[SPEAKER_00]: It is because you don't know where the line for your responsibility and your actual doing is.

336
00:30:58,387 --> 00:31:03,870
[SPEAKER_00]: But, say, you know, you start doing it, you put up with it, it becomes more freeing.

337
00:31:04,857 --> 00:31:16,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you realize, okay, if it's not rooted in those things, I don't need to police it, I don't need to worry about it, I don't need to be catching moment to moment so my mindfulness doesn't slip and whatnot.

338
00:31:17,344 --> 00:31:17,804
[SPEAKER_00]: I can do it.

339
00:31:19,625 --> 00:31:25,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if the doubt comes up, which you will initially recognize, okay, now I have a choice in regard to the doubt.

340
00:31:25,488 --> 00:31:26,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Before, I wasn't aware of it.

341
00:31:27,472 --> 00:31:34,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Before, whenever hindrance came up, it would pressure me and I would just get caught up in this cycle and I couldn't stop until I was just tired, basically, and forgot about it.

342
00:31:35,197 --> 00:31:35,818
[SPEAKER_00]: But now I know.

343
00:31:36,278 --> 00:31:39,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what comes up through that animal.

344
00:31:40,581 --> 00:31:42,742
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind of my mind, what brings up.

345
00:31:43,783 --> 00:31:46,044
[SPEAKER_00]: My choice in regard to it remains unchanged.

346
00:31:46,064 --> 00:31:47,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't get closer, doesn't go further.

347
00:31:47,685 --> 00:31:48,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Remains at the same level.

348
00:31:49,487 --> 00:31:51,048
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that door I keep talking about.

349
00:31:51,068 --> 00:31:51,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't open the door.

350
00:31:51,788 --> 00:31:54,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what's trying to get in or trying to pressure you to open it.

351
00:31:55,646 --> 00:31:58,849
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you recognize that, the more confident you become in it.

352
00:31:58,869 --> 00:31:59,429
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, yeah.

353
00:32:00,210 --> 00:32:05,735
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any state of mind, and that's again the satipaṭṭhāna done rightly.

354
00:32:05,795 --> 00:32:07,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Depressed mind has depressed mind.

355
00:32:07,997 --> 00:32:10,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, I'm not accepting the choices that depressed mind is offering.

356
00:32:11,019 --> 00:32:17,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Elated mind, distracted mind, any state of mind, elevated, doesn't matter.

357
00:32:18,266 --> 00:32:18,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Fine.

358
00:32:18,646 --> 00:32:19,627
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it as such.

359
00:32:20,524 --> 00:32:29,868
[SPEAKER_00]: But the recognition of the line of the choice and the cetan and intention remains unchanged and that's the gateway, that's my door to basically training the animal.

360
00:32:31,389 --> 00:32:36,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you have all the other advanced, the actual, you know, higher mind training.

361
00:32:37,530 --> 00:32:49,807
[SPEAKER_00]: As the suttas described, which is, you know, those perceptions presented to that mind, contemplating this sufficiently enough, so that the mind stops resisting, stops trying to outrun, and stops reacting to them.

362
00:32:51,071 --> 00:32:53,834
[SPEAKER_00]: But all that can be done once you actually see the mind.

363
00:32:53,894 --> 00:32:58,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like... again, people can... people would be doing it in the same sense.

364
00:32:58,098 --> 00:33:02,642
[SPEAKER_00]: A foolish cook gets all new ideas because he came across new recipe books, i.e.

365
00:33:02,702 --> 00:33:05,605
[SPEAKER_00]: new suttas, and then he's just blindly doing this and that.

366
00:33:05,805 --> 00:33:07,507
[SPEAKER_00]: One week this, another retreat that.

367
00:33:08,108 --> 00:33:11,451
[SPEAKER_00]: No internal connection and recognition to how that affects the mind.

368
00:33:17,770 --> 00:33:23,537
[SPEAKER_00]: So, another aspect here, although I kind of covered all the questions pretty much, but anyway...

369
00:33:23,557 --> 00:33:24,938
[SPEAKER_00]: I can go quickly through it.

370
00:33:25,098 --> 00:33:35,430
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can I recognize the difference between my experience of the external objects and, as in, you know, engaging with the senses and so on, and the underlying state of mind that is concurrently enduring in relation to them?

371
00:33:36,391 --> 00:33:37,072
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words,

372
00:33:38,738 --> 00:33:53,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I at least able to some extent to not be completely absorbed in what the senses offer, the sense objects, and maintain some notion of the context of the background in relation to it?

373
00:33:53,928 --> 00:33:55,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where your intentions are.

374
00:33:55,609 --> 00:34:02,534
[SPEAKER_00]: You're never going to see intentions in sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, because that's why you're looking at them, because of the intentions not seen.

375
00:34:04,175 --> 00:34:07,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Intentions not seen are sending you in the directions of these sense objects.

376
00:34:12,870 --> 00:34:14,591
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the recollection of the body within the body.

377
00:34:16,971 --> 00:34:18,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, how do you do it?

378
00:34:20,692 --> 00:34:21,773
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the body within the body?

379
00:34:21,793 --> 00:34:22,913
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the mind within the mind?

380
00:34:26,254 --> 00:34:26,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we...

381
00:34:26,974 --> 00:34:27,894
[SPEAKER_00]: I answered it, obviously.

382
00:34:29,075 --> 00:34:29,595
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the animal.

383
00:34:29,675 --> 00:34:30,555
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same with the body.

384
00:34:32,436 --> 00:34:37,577
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you look at the body, any idea that you have of it, it's not the actual body.

385
00:34:38,598 --> 00:34:39,678
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a body within that.

386
00:34:40,975 --> 00:34:48,761
[SPEAKER_00]: The body, they can only be seen indirectly on account of purifying all of your intentions in relation to that body.

387
00:34:51,023 --> 00:34:51,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Where are those intentions?

388
00:34:51,964 --> 00:34:52,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Right there.

389
00:34:52,785 --> 00:34:56,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even there and then, when you're asking yourself, there is intention to do it.

390
00:34:56,387 --> 00:34:59,270
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ask yourself, okay, why am I asking myself this?

391
00:34:59,550 --> 00:35:01,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I doing what Ajahn Mahārāj said to do now?

392
00:35:04,674 --> 00:35:05,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I have faith or something.

393
00:35:06,396 --> 00:35:06,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

394
00:35:07,660 --> 00:35:10,263
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it, the aspect that inspires faith in me?

395
00:35:10,803 --> 00:35:11,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just take it for granted.

396
00:35:12,024 --> 00:35:14,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing should be taken for granted.

397
00:35:17,109 --> 00:35:21,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Because to the extent you take things for granted, to that extent you have obscured your own intentions.

398
00:35:22,895 --> 00:35:27,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, again, it goes without saying, it's not going to be perfectly clean overnight.

399
00:35:28,340 --> 00:35:29,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Suddenly all intentions are pure.

400
00:35:31,113 --> 00:35:32,093
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's a training.

401
00:35:32,313 --> 00:35:34,874
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the effort that requires repetition and everything else.

402
00:35:35,014 --> 00:35:37,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you better start doing it.

403
00:35:38,475 --> 00:35:42,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even things that, yeah, are self-evident.

404
00:35:42,716 --> 00:35:43,176
[SPEAKER_00]: This is true.

405
00:35:43,216 --> 00:35:43,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

406
00:35:44,096 --> 00:35:44,616
[SPEAKER_00]: This is right.

407
00:35:45,236 --> 00:35:45,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, why?

408
00:35:46,256 --> 00:35:49,617
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's right and if I understand it as right, well, I'll be at least a sotāpanna.

409
00:35:49,637 --> 00:35:51,057
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why am I not a sotāpanna?

410
00:35:53,298 --> 00:35:54,498
[SPEAKER_00]: And it doesn't matter if you're a householder.

411
00:35:56,739 --> 00:35:58,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's not an excuse either.

412
00:36:01,705 --> 00:36:09,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Because no amount of household life, external science, as I said, can come in between you and your own intention.

413
00:36:09,312 --> 00:36:10,313
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your own doing.

414
00:36:12,595 --> 00:36:22,284
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your intentions are not pure enough, because of which you're not seeing the signs of the mind, because of which you're not understanding the Four Noble Truths that are not Sotāpanna, it's your own doing, on that level.

415
00:36:23,565 --> 00:36:25,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Your own choices are still

416
00:36:27,677 --> 00:36:31,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Made in a way that's actually obstructing seeing the animal.

417
00:36:34,583 --> 00:36:39,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, start making the right ones and even if you don't want to become a sotāpanna, you will.

418
00:36:40,493 --> 00:36:42,415
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it needs to be done on that mental level.

419
00:36:42,455 --> 00:36:46,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just, okay, I made the choice, keep the precepts and I'll just blindly stick to it.

420
00:36:46,859 --> 00:36:57,088
[SPEAKER_00]: But mentally, when things come up, when the possibility to choose rightly and say no to such thoughts, say no to such proliferations, I tolerate it.

421
00:36:57,249 --> 00:36:58,890
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't make those... that effort.

422
00:36:58,950 --> 00:36:59,811
[SPEAKER_00]: I just...

423
00:37:00,652 --> 00:37:03,134
[SPEAKER_00]: I know I won't do it by the body or speech, so this is fine.

424
00:37:03,775 --> 00:37:06,377
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like again, the roots remain infected.

425
00:37:10,172 --> 00:37:17,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So... so, yeah, like, when the Sutta talks about, you know, the inner sign and stuff, it's... it's... it's just what I mean by context.

426
00:37:17,714 --> 00:37:26,557
[SPEAKER_00]: When I often say you develop the right context and then you sustain that context in regard to your engagement with the world, that's going to be inevitable.

427
00:37:26,577 --> 00:37:29,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Sight, sounds, mouth, taste, touches, things having to do and so on.

428
00:37:30,018 --> 00:37:32,038
[SPEAKER_00]: But the context does not need to suffer.

429
00:37:32,138 --> 00:37:38,460
[SPEAKER_00]: If the context starts to suffer, then you need to restrain yourself and pull yourself back until you can re-establish the context.

430
00:37:39,228 --> 00:37:42,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Context of, well, I will not lose the sight of my intentions.

431
00:37:43,329 --> 00:37:55,553
[SPEAKER_00]: I will certainly not give in to the, as I said, the wide basis of, like, carelessness, lust, and so on, because, again, it would fuel the mind in the wrong way, and then I would have a lot more to undo.

432
00:37:57,455 --> 00:38:04,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And just in case, I don't know, for somebody maybe it's not clear, the intentions are not these momentary things that, oh, I just missed it now.

433
00:38:05,697 --> 00:38:07,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Came, I made it to choice.

434
00:38:07,178 --> 00:38:07,318
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

435
00:38:08,078 --> 00:38:12,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention can last for five days, if the choice lasts for five days.

436
00:38:15,340 --> 00:38:17,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, you are abstaining from choice for five days.

437
00:38:18,161 --> 00:38:21,502
[SPEAKER_00]: That same intention, same state of mind is there for five days.

438
00:38:23,642 --> 00:38:25,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever you are aware of it, that's where it's there.

439
00:38:27,842 --> 00:38:28,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you do?

440
00:38:29,643 --> 00:38:35,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why would you be even looking at the momentary movements and changes?

441
00:38:35,985 --> 00:38:37,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Only because you don't know where the emphasis is.

442
00:38:37,786 --> 00:38:41,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Only because... again... See, the foolish cook

443
00:38:42,928 --> 00:38:50,011
[SPEAKER_00]: You can kind of tell the king is not pleased, but still doesn't have a clue why, as to, like, what has he done right or wrong, or...

444
00:38:50,591 --> 00:38:54,133
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's just getting paranoid about everything he does now.

445
00:38:55,513 --> 00:38:58,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everything becomes a problem, basically, because he doesn't know where the problem is.

446
00:38:59,855 --> 00:39:04,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me now just monitor everything, but you're still monitoring it at a completely wrong place.

447
00:39:05,710 --> 00:39:10,851
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem was never in things that you can perceive through your senses.

448
00:39:10,891 --> 00:39:14,412
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem was always in the intentions, your own choices.

449
00:39:15,713 --> 00:39:20,854
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have an intention for five days because you're abstaining from the choice, but the mind keeps bringing it back.

450
00:39:21,794 --> 00:39:22,235
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you do?

451
00:39:24,715 --> 00:39:26,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you bear that sense of confinement?

452
00:39:28,796 --> 00:39:30,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Nagging, weighing down, increasing in weight?

453
00:39:32,773 --> 00:39:37,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Even saying, yeah, it's weird, like, I shouldn't be thinking about it for five days.

454
00:39:37,415 --> 00:39:40,057
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just a small thing.

455
00:39:40,077 --> 00:39:41,578
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just a small thing.

456
00:39:42,458 --> 00:39:43,699
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a hassle to think about it.

457
00:39:44,059 --> 00:39:45,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, let me just act out of it.

458
00:39:47,040 --> 00:39:55,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me just psychologize it until I find a pleasing solution mentally, or let me go and say something if it was concerning others, or let me do something.

459
00:39:56,545 --> 00:39:59,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you made a wrong choice.

460
00:39:59,567 --> 00:40:01,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you chose to give in

461
00:40:02,747 --> 00:40:07,469
[SPEAKER_00]: To the pressure of the animal, to the pressure of the untrained mind, and did a random thing as a result of it.

462
00:40:09,910 --> 00:40:11,691
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the choice lasts five days, what do you do?

463
00:40:12,171 --> 00:40:12,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

464
00:40:12,531 --> 00:40:15,953
[SPEAKER_00]: You still don't make... well, you do the right abstaining from the wrong choice.

465
00:40:18,054 --> 00:40:20,195
[SPEAKER_00]: If the choice then lasts for three months, what do you do?

466
00:40:20,655 --> 00:40:20,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

467
00:40:22,256 --> 00:40:25,277
[SPEAKER_00]: But three months have to keep this ambiguity, this...

468
00:40:25,937 --> 00:40:27,798
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it will all go away, literally.

469
00:40:28,904 --> 00:40:32,986
[SPEAKER_00]: This mental problem and the pressure would go away if I just go and talk to that person.

470
00:40:34,306 --> 00:40:35,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it would go away.

471
00:40:36,127 --> 00:40:39,169
[SPEAKER_00]: But what would also go away is your own mind with it.

472
00:40:39,249 --> 00:40:40,589
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you would obstruct it.

473
00:40:40,969 --> 00:40:41,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

474
00:40:41,370 --> 00:40:54,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for you, doesn't matter if it's not a problem for that person, doesn't matter if it's not a problem for the world, doesn't matter if it's not a problem for any system, belief system you have, it's a problem for you because it's rooted in a wrong intention.

475
00:40:54,495 --> 00:40:58,117
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in restlessness, it's rooted in worry, it's rooted in hindrances.

476
00:40:59,585 --> 00:41:05,087
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you prioritize your ideas of how things should be and how the world should operate and how the mind should be in response?

477
00:41:05,528 --> 00:41:13,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you prioritize the actual live state of the king that's telling you... and you don't know why.

478
00:41:14,191 --> 00:41:17,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're just doing random stuff until, okay, he's pacified now.

479
00:41:18,413 --> 00:41:19,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't have to deal with him.

480
00:41:19,854 --> 00:41:20,314
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

481
00:41:20,614 --> 00:41:22,595
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to be at the mercy of the animal.

482
00:41:22,655 --> 00:41:23,515
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to train it.

483
00:41:25,096 --> 00:41:27,417
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only way to train it, the only way to speak to it,

484
00:41:28,454 --> 00:41:35,558
[SPEAKER_00]: It's through recognizing the level, the layer of the background where your intentions are, and then not making the wrong ones.

485
00:41:36,239 --> 00:41:48,707
[SPEAKER_00]: And I always give that example, because it's like a kind of a good, simple textbook, conveys it well, which is like, even in the monasteries, like, you know, I don't know how many times I've seen that,

486
00:41:49,467 --> 00:41:53,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, everybody knows, you know, the suttas sufficiently enough.

487
00:41:53,969 --> 00:41:57,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe not every monk is an expert or read them all, but they know sufficiently enough.

488
00:41:57,210 --> 00:42:04,753
[SPEAKER_00]: And they certainly know the suttas, you know, where the Buddha said, like, for example, you shouldn't say anything out of anger.

489
00:42:05,173 --> 00:42:06,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what it is.

490
00:42:07,294 --> 00:42:10,935
[SPEAKER_00]: If for you it's rooted in intentional ill-will, you shouldn't say it.

491
00:42:11,255 --> 00:42:14,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's the most beneficial thing for the world.

492
00:42:15,697 --> 00:42:16,797
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is your intention.

493
00:42:16,837 --> 00:42:18,138
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is your state of mind.

494
00:42:19,185 --> 00:42:27,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the monk wouldn't say until, basically, the anger has subsided, if the thing is not beneficial for both, and so on.

495
00:42:27,929 --> 00:42:29,850
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point was, everybody knows that.

496
00:42:30,990 --> 00:42:38,014
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, like, okay, you know, nobody will punch or physically attack anybody in the monastery.

497
00:42:38,794 --> 00:42:40,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody will, like...

498
00:42:40,415 --> 00:42:47,318
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it might happen, but, say, most people wouldn't, you know, go really far in, like, an angry speech, would be able to restrain it.

499
00:42:48,207 --> 00:42:53,408
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, you know, mentally, like, hmm... it doesn't seem like a big deal.

500
00:42:54,548 --> 00:42:58,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I know it's a problem, but, yeah, you know, it's okay.

501
00:42:58,549 --> 00:43:10,972
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, say, somebody does something annoying, junior monk does something annoying, and often you would see, like, a senior monk, you know, if he's maybe, like, really annoyed with it, he wouldn't do anything there and then.

502
00:43:11,753 --> 00:43:20,698
[SPEAKER_00]: But, say, next day, or two or three days later, same annoyance, by the way, the same choice, the same citta, pushing the same agenda on you.

503
00:43:22,659 --> 00:43:25,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, you know, three days now, three days it's been eating me.

504
00:43:27,021 --> 00:43:27,462
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do?

505
00:43:29,863 --> 00:43:41,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I find an external justification to say something, or do I say there is no an external sign that can replace the internal recognition of the citta and the nimittas and the characteristics?

506
00:43:43,090 --> 00:43:45,172
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that's your value, you wouldn't do anything.

507
00:43:45,232 --> 00:43:52,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if six months later, that same annoyance in regard to what somebody did, still there, you're still not acting out of it.

508
00:43:53,661 --> 00:43:54,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And it might be.

509
00:43:54,202 --> 00:43:54,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Who knows?

510
00:43:54,642 --> 00:43:55,483
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be for months.

511
00:43:55,763 --> 00:43:56,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Depending on, like...

512
00:43:58,146 --> 00:44:01,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Depending on the mind of your mind, that you have no say in.

513
00:44:02,050 --> 00:44:02,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it like this?

514
00:44:03,011 --> 00:44:03,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

515
00:44:03,492 --> 00:44:04,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't really matter, really.

516
00:44:06,867 --> 00:44:07,468
[SPEAKER_00]: It's irrelevant.

517
00:44:08,248 --> 00:44:15,995
[SPEAKER_00]: So, most of the monks that I remember, most of the people, basically, would find one reason or the other to justify and then say it.

518
00:44:16,716 --> 00:44:27,246
[SPEAKER_00]: They would not say it through, like, anger or something, but it would always be... they would make a policy in the monastery or something or do a dhamma talk where it's just about that.

519
00:44:27,846 --> 00:44:38,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to preemptively address that, and basically, which becomes a form of acting out of that mind, because that uncertainty, abstaining from the wrong choice, it's increasing its weight.

520
00:44:38,598 --> 00:44:41,602
[SPEAKER_00]: I can do it one day, two days, three days, maybe a week, but after a while,

521
00:44:42,865 --> 00:44:45,826
[SPEAKER_00]: While it's increasing its weight, it's kind of decreasing its value.

522
00:44:46,006 --> 00:44:46,827
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it seems.

523
00:44:46,907 --> 00:44:48,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not a big deal.

524
00:44:48,788 --> 00:44:50,228
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's also for the good of the monastery.

525
00:44:50,268 --> 00:44:52,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what he did was factually wrong.

526
00:44:52,829 --> 00:44:53,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Other people might do it.

527
00:44:53,870 --> 00:45:00,953
[SPEAKER_00]: And then everything we worked for, this great dhamma, and the monastery we built, and inspiration we provide, everything will go to ruin.

528
00:45:01,473 --> 00:45:05,775
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your own crazy citta, untrained animal, trying to get you to act.

529
00:45:06,995 --> 00:45:08,456
[SPEAKER_00]: It will tell you, hey, open the gate for me.

530
00:45:08,956 --> 00:45:11,017
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say, no, I'm practicing restraint.

531
00:45:12,114 --> 00:45:12,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay.

532
00:45:14,655 --> 00:45:20,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you know, if you don't open the gates, things can happen out there, you know.

533
00:45:21,237 --> 00:45:23,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Other, other, other animals can come.

534
00:45:24,077 --> 00:45:25,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Issues can develop.

535
00:45:25,238 --> 00:45:26,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you sure you can live with that?

536
00:45:27,938 --> 00:45:29,079
[SPEAKER_00]: No, yeah, I'm not opening the gate.

537
00:45:29,119 --> 00:45:29,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, really?

538
00:45:29,479 --> 00:45:30,279
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not opening the gate?

539
00:45:30,299 --> 00:45:33,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you're not opening the gate, we're going to have a problem in here, plus the other.

540
00:45:33,680 --> 00:45:35,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, okay, okay, I'll open the gate.

541
00:45:36,081 --> 00:45:37,381
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much how it works.

542
00:45:38,408 --> 00:45:58,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if people wouldn't open the gate, regardless of the irrationally... kind of... irrational... accumulation of the irrational, like increasingly more irrational scenarios that your mind is throwing at you, yet you still don't open the gate, you still don't make the right choice, you are on the track to be a sotāpanna, even if you don't want to become one.

543
00:45:58,896 --> 00:46:02,998
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I said nobody can accidentally become a sotāpanna, because nobody will accidentally...

544
00:46:04,228 --> 00:46:11,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Stick to this, which, as I said, it completely loses the value of... oh, but it's just... it's done for the good reason.

545
00:46:12,475 --> 00:46:16,479
[SPEAKER_00]: While not doing it, it increases the weight that your mind threatens you.

546
00:46:17,219 --> 00:46:19,521
[SPEAKER_00]: And you just choose to... no, I'm still not going to do it.

547
00:46:19,922 --> 00:46:20,782
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm still not going to do it.

548
00:46:21,823 --> 00:46:23,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody will do that accidentally.

549
00:46:23,204 --> 00:46:27,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Only persons that have understood sutta sufficiently

550
00:46:28,300 --> 00:46:31,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Understood the importance of not acting out by body, speech and mind.

551
00:46:31,502 --> 00:46:38,927
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll stick to it, regardless of the storm that citta is cooking up, trying to scare you, so to open the gate, so it can go back to its wilderness.

552
00:46:43,410 --> 00:46:46,171
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody is sotāpanna, it's because they made the right effort.

553
00:46:46,532 --> 00:46:48,673
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody is not sotāpanna, it's because they haven't made the right effort.

554
00:46:49,153 --> 00:46:54,897
[SPEAKER_00]: They might have made all the effort in the world, in all the directions, but not the right one, not on the right level.

555
00:47:01,358 --> 00:47:05,340
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I kind of answered all the questions, really.

556
00:47:12,463 --> 00:47:15,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, there's like one practical thing I received.

557
00:47:16,305 --> 00:47:23,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person asked, how should one practice while working or speaking or engaging with others?

558
00:47:27,656 --> 00:47:34,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, when a person has to attend to things, has to discuss stuff, has to plan and so on.

559
00:47:35,563 --> 00:47:37,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, what do I do, in other words?

560
00:47:42,109 --> 00:47:43,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, everything we just described, really.

561
00:47:43,711 --> 00:47:45,893
[SPEAKER_00]: And in other talks, well, you protect the context.

562
00:47:47,758 --> 00:48:15,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, say, say if your practice was before revolving around a bit kind of too much on the cushioned environment, you know, again, I remember, like, the places, monasteries around the world, where, like, even having to, like, you know, not by the place, but there were monks that even having to, like, sweep leaves or do stuff, any form of, like, like a physical exertion is just too disruptive for any mental equilibrium.

563
00:48:16,080 --> 00:48:18,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's kind of worse than a householder, in a way.

564
00:48:18,542 --> 00:48:22,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Householders don't have that privilege, don't have that luxury.

565
00:48:24,906 --> 00:48:29,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, you can be protecting yourself all you want, the mind will be bombarded with stuff.

566
00:48:30,249 --> 00:48:39,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you need to withdraw yourself to some extent, but the point is that it comes down to that, like, as I said,

567
00:48:40,732 --> 00:48:47,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I might be afraid of doing it because I'm dependent on the cushion environment, but now I learned enough that those things shouldn't be a problem.

568
00:48:48,558 --> 00:48:49,799
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do I engage with them?

569
00:48:50,400 --> 00:48:52,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I keep an eye on the background.

570
00:48:52,541 --> 00:48:55,684
[SPEAKER_00]: I keep an eye on my state of mind, my intentions.

571
00:48:55,704 --> 00:49:03,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, there might be states of fear and anxiety and uncertainty coming up because things are not in my control, you know, he can say this, she can say that.

572
00:49:04,050 --> 00:49:05,552
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm accepting the outcomes of that.

573
00:49:05,672 --> 00:49:07,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody says something,

574
00:49:08,387 --> 00:49:12,648
[SPEAKER_00]: That might make my cheetah just jump quickly.

575
00:49:12,668 --> 00:49:14,109
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll focus on not acting out.

576
00:49:14,369 --> 00:49:19,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter if that cheetah hurts then for next seven days.

577
00:49:19,210 --> 00:49:19,690
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll bear that.

578
00:49:19,830 --> 00:49:22,731
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the outcome of the walking in the right direction.

579
00:49:22,771 --> 00:49:25,472
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the pretty much how... how would it feel?

580
00:49:25,512 --> 00:49:28,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine, how would it feel to go with the grain?

581
00:49:29,373 --> 00:49:31,473
[SPEAKER_00]: How would it feel to go against the grain?

582
00:49:32,994 --> 00:49:34,474
[SPEAKER_00]: How would it feel to swim with the river?

583
00:49:35,455 --> 00:49:37,035
[SPEAKER_00]: How would it feel to swim against the river?

584
00:49:39,366 --> 00:49:42,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the Buddha said, dhamma goes against the grain.

585
00:49:44,627 --> 00:49:46,388
[SPEAKER_00]: You swim basically against the floods.

586
00:49:48,168 --> 00:49:52,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's gonna feel like... uncomfortable.

587
00:49:52,749 --> 00:49:54,389
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna feel like, oh, it's not right.

588
00:49:54,489 --> 00:49:55,730
[SPEAKER_00]: If only I could resolve it mentally.

589
00:49:55,750 --> 00:49:57,050
[SPEAKER_00]: If only I could find justification.

590
00:49:57,090 --> 00:50:05,752
[SPEAKER_00]: If only I could, you know, use psychoanalysis to find why this is so, so to give me a sense of, oh, now I know why it is, so it's fine.

591
00:50:06,592 --> 00:50:07,353
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

592
00:50:07,913 --> 00:50:13,157
[SPEAKER_00]: It really doesn't matter whether it's because something happened in this life or previous or nothing really happened.

593
00:50:13,998 --> 00:50:15,359
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the mind of your own mind.

594
00:50:16,160 --> 00:50:23,225
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't go worrying around why some random stranger has a preference of one kind as opposed to the other.

595
00:50:23,806 --> 00:50:25,667
[SPEAKER_00]: You really wouldn't... you wouldn't worry about that.

596
00:50:26,688 --> 00:50:29,210
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you worry about the preferences of your own citta?

597
00:50:29,270 --> 00:50:30,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Where they come from?

598
00:50:31,172 --> 00:50:34,154
[SPEAKER_00]: You should only worry about whether you are giving in to them or not.

599
00:50:35,512 --> 00:50:43,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you have to engage with the world, when you have to engage with the things,

600
00:50:47,843 --> 00:50:55,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtue, sense-restraint and the context on account of that, and then mental, as I said, mental choices, as much as obviously you are aware at the time.

601
00:50:56,247 --> 00:51:06,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you might be distracted and so on, and you might be mentally, like, accepting irritation or this or that, but at least you are recognizing that that is what's happening.

602
00:51:08,623 --> 00:51:19,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And the broader context is keeping it all together, which is, well, I will not say anything or do anything by the body, regardless of what's happening to me, so to speak, how distracted I am, and so on.

603
00:51:19,810 --> 00:51:34,940
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you do fail, then recognize, okay, that was... that was too much, at least for now, so next time I should be even more careful before becoming that distracted, so that then I acted out by body or by speech.

604
00:51:35,000 --> 00:51:37,242
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, you can never be

605
00:51:38,623 --> 00:51:41,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So distracted to become unaware of the choice.

606
00:51:41,945 --> 00:51:47,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can be distracted so you don't see the choice when it comes up or when it starts coming up.

607
00:51:47,189 --> 00:51:48,570
[SPEAKER_00]: You only see when it's in front of you.

608
00:51:48,870 --> 00:51:49,731
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still a choice.

609
00:51:50,572 --> 00:51:53,234
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of greed, aversion, delusion can make a choice for you.

610
00:51:54,034 --> 00:52:04,982
[SPEAKER_00]: If choices were to naturally and automatically follow, you know, what Abhidhamma and commentaries think, from greed, aversion, delusion and the states of mind of that, arahantri would never be possible.

611
00:52:06,468 --> 00:52:09,409
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be a completely self-perpetuating thing and you couldn't come in between.

612
00:52:10,670 --> 00:52:13,451
[SPEAKER_00]: But every single time, cetanā is always on you.

613
00:52:15,812 --> 00:52:21,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, indirectly, you are responsible for the states of your citta in that broadest sense.

614
00:52:21,994 --> 00:52:24,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Not immediately, but in the broadest sense.

615
00:52:33,299 --> 00:52:35,940
[SPEAKER_00]: So, kind of related to this question was another one that I received, which is

616
00:52:36,908 --> 00:52:50,237
[SPEAKER_00]: So, say you are engaged with tasks, duties, work and so on, and things get a bit all unclear and muddy, and you lost your internal clarity and peace, things are disturbed, like, feels like a jungle.

617
00:52:50,417 --> 00:52:51,137
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you do there?

618
00:52:51,438 --> 00:52:53,659
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, do you stop, start counting your breath and stuff?

619
00:52:55,360 --> 00:53:01,164
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, again, you might do those little things to help you cope, but you don't need to do any of that.

620
00:53:01,224 --> 00:53:01,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

621
00:53:01,504 --> 00:53:02,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't matter.

622
00:53:03,368 --> 00:53:05,229
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, what state of mind there is.

623
00:53:05,289 --> 00:53:08,430
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's a jungle, there's no clarity in my mind.

624
00:53:08,450 --> 00:53:11,891
[SPEAKER_00]: This morning everything was so clear and calm and spacious.

625
00:53:11,991 --> 00:53:13,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Now it's just so cluttered.

626
00:53:13,272 --> 00:53:13,952
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all over the place.

627
00:53:14,472 --> 00:53:16,233
[SPEAKER_00]: What's your intention in regard to that?

628
00:53:17,393 --> 00:53:19,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Know that the mind is contained.

629
00:53:20,628 --> 00:53:25,371
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, not seeing that, you worry about, oh, it's like a jungle, the mind is cluttered, da-da.

630
00:53:25,611 --> 00:53:30,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, so you don't know the cluttered mind as cluttered mind.

631
00:53:30,214 --> 00:53:34,597
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to change the cluttered mind because it doesn't feel right because of your ideas or feelings.

632
00:53:35,518 --> 00:53:40,061
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to change it into something else that suits your preferences better.

633
00:53:40,181 --> 00:53:41,021
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no, no, no.

634
00:53:41,181 --> 00:53:43,503
[SPEAKER_00]: You are not the reference point.

635
00:53:43,563 --> 00:53:44,984
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is the reference point.

636
00:53:45,604 --> 00:53:46,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, King doesn't like that.

637
00:53:46,765 --> 00:53:49,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll just keep serving it until he starts liking it.

638
00:53:55,998 --> 00:53:57,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I don't like when he doesn't like it.

639
00:53:58,399 --> 00:54:00,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'll just force him into it, so to speak.

640
00:54:00,700 --> 00:54:09,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your mind is cluttered, gets busy, feels like a jungle, unreachable, not as peaceful, not as comfortable.

641
00:54:10,425 --> 00:54:12,306
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the state of mind that you know as such.

642
00:54:12,766 --> 00:54:14,647
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's like, oh, so what do I do in regard to that?

643
00:54:16,187 --> 00:54:17,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Without acting out, trying to change it.

644
00:54:21,131 --> 00:54:28,054
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's, again, that's how immediately you re-establish the context, by recognizing what your intention is in regard to any state of mind.

645
00:54:30,256 --> 00:54:46,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would it then be right to say that our intention is usually deeply rooted or connected with the feelings and our need to get rid or have more or distract ourselves from it, from the feeling.

646
00:54:47,977 --> 00:54:52,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, yeah, like feeling, perception, intention, as the Sutta says, it's inseparable.

647
00:54:53,959 --> 00:55:08,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might start with, say, like more specific intentions, or I want to say this or do that, but ultimately, yes, you start going down the pathway of intentions, it's gonna come converge onto where things are felt.

648
00:55:09,224 --> 00:55:16,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're gonna start to recognize that simply because something is felt, agreeably,

649
00:55:18,222 --> 00:55:24,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Not even overly pleasant, you already have intention kind of towards it, to accept it and so on.

650
00:55:24,408 --> 00:55:31,835
[SPEAKER_00]: But those are subtle things, you know, they can become, say, they can be discerned in broad strokes.

651
00:55:32,076 --> 00:55:37,901
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't really need to worry about, like, pinpoint on the level of feeling or something, because

652
00:55:38,842 --> 00:55:49,255
[SPEAKER_00]: There are bigger impurities that are obstructing the citta way before that, which is other acts by body, speech and mind, habits that you're still tolerating while you know they're rooted in hindrances.

653
00:55:51,891 --> 00:55:53,352
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, deal with that first.

654
00:55:53,952 --> 00:55:58,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the time will come when you recognize the subtle leanings of your intention.

655
00:55:58,094 --> 00:56:02,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Fatigue against the discomfort, leaning towards comfort, and so on.

656
00:56:03,537 --> 00:56:07,179
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, broadly, everything you do will fall into these three categories.

657
00:56:07,519 --> 00:56:14,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Either for more pleasure, less pain, or ignorance of the... ignoring, distracting yourself from neither pleasant or unpleasant.

658
00:56:16,862 --> 00:56:22,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though the king is pleased with the right dishes, it seems that the mind is, at least initially, is pleased with the right choices.

659
00:56:22,424 --> 00:56:27,065
[SPEAKER_00]: What would be the corresponding measure of rightness in the case of the citta?

660
00:56:30,086 --> 00:56:33,827
[SPEAKER_00]: The corresponding... well, it would be basically the absence of dukkha.

661
00:56:36,028 --> 00:56:43,030
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much why the four noble truths follow for the one that sees the signs of the mind.

662
00:56:44,268 --> 00:56:54,916
[SPEAKER_00]: which follows the one that's been training rightly in secluding himself from, internally, from greed, aversion, distraction and the hindrances.

663
00:56:56,417 --> 00:57:00,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it becomes... that's the verification.

664
00:57:01,221 --> 00:57:08,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not like, as I said, you're gonna have some grand reveal, like parting of the curtains and the truth shows itself.

665
00:57:11,575 --> 00:57:16,080
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna be like, you are unable to suffer, because the mind doesn't move in the way it used to move.

666
00:57:17,681 --> 00:57:18,922
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't latch, it doesn't bite.

667
00:57:20,324 --> 00:57:21,805
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't, and you know it.

668
00:57:23,087 --> 00:57:27,351
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it's like, oh, I live like a lotus in the filth, untouched by it.

669
00:57:28,332 --> 00:57:32,456
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I live in everything that used to cause issues and suffering, but untouched by it.

670
00:57:33,455 --> 00:57:35,336
[SPEAKER_00]: And Sotāpanna already gets the taste of that.

671
00:57:35,536 --> 00:57:44,001
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he's certain, destined for awakening and has unshakable confidence in the fact that, well, knows that dhamma works.

672
00:57:45,322 --> 00:57:48,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if the whole world comes and tells you, no, the Buddha was actually wrong.

673
00:57:48,303 --> 00:57:49,864
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, the suttas were discovered.

674
00:57:49,904 --> 00:57:51,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Turns out somebody wrote them 400 years ago.

675
00:57:51,985 --> 00:57:53,046
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, there's a proof.

676
00:57:53,166 --> 00:57:55,987
[SPEAKER_00]: And they did the laser scanning and it was all discovered.

677
00:57:56,027 --> 00:57:56,788
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all hoax.

678
00:57:57,685 --> 00:57:59,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that was a lucky hoax.

679
00:57:59,827 --> 00:58:05,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by doing that, you're untouched by suffering internally, and nobody can, like... And it's not even a conviction.

680
00:58:05,892 --> 00:58:15,080
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, because I convinced myself, I'll never think... No, it's literally, like, everything stays the same, yet animal cannot make you suffer anymore.

681
00:58:16,782 --> 00:58:16,982
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

682
00:58:18,160 --> 00:58:23,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you would know that the dhamma is actually right, even if, say, the historical Buddha never existed.

683
00:58:23,524 --> 00:58:30,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, of course, it's impossible, because otherwise, like, you know, who could accidentally stumble upon the right instruction, unless somebody did it themselves.

684
00:58:31,269 --> 00:58:39,054
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I'm just saying, hypothetically speaking, even if they prove that all the suttas are, like, written by somebody, it's like, well, that somebody freed himself from suffering.

685
00:58:40,715 --> 00:58:43,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, on account of that somebody's instructions,

686
00:58:44,508 --> 00:58:45,489
[SPEAKER_00]: I trained my own mind.

687
00:58:45,729 --> 00:58:46,750
[SPEAKER_00]: I prepared the elephant.

688
00:58:46,810 --> 00:58:47,691
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't flinch now.

689
00:58:47,811 --> 00:58:51,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, the measure of it is there is no suffering.

690
00:58:51,874 --> 00:58:52,434
[SPEAKER_00]: There is peace.

691
00:58:53,015 --> 00:58:56,838
[SPEAKER_00]: And, obviously, you value that, but citta values that as well.

692
00:58:57,358 --> 00:58:58,860
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's your duty to train it.

693
00:58:59,140 --> 00:59:00,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, same like with a wild animal.

694
00:59:01,282 --> 00:59:05,165
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't know what it is actually good for it.

695
00:59:05,205 --> 00:59:06,506
[SPEAKER_00]: It wants to go to the wilderness.

696
00:59:06,526 --> 00:59:07,847
[SPEAKER_00]: It wants to get into fights.

697
00:59:07,887 --> 00:59:09,008
[SPEAKER_00]: It wants to go and mate.

698
00:59:09,048 --> 00:59:11,590
[SPEAKER_00]: It wants to do this, because it doesn't know

699
00:59:13,183 --> 00:59:20,367
[SPEAKER_00]: That those things are actually not offering peace, are rooted in suffering and perpetuation of that thing.

700
00:59:20,807 --> 00:59:24,189
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to show it otherwise and when it's eased, then it's liberated.

701
00:59:24,349 --> 00:59:26,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you wouldn't have to do anything more.

702
00:59:26,850 --> 00:59:32,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you say the hindrances are that tiny felt need to do something to be at ease?

703
00:59:34,415 --> 00:59:37,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And we need to restrain and recognize that felt need as a burden.

704
00:59:37,717 --> 00:59:38,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Slavery.

705
00:59:38,937 --> 00:59:40,178
[SPEAKER_00]: It finally helped to investigate this way.

706
00:59:40,378 --> 00:59:44,540
[SPEAKER_00]: If my mind was truly free of hindrances, I would be at ease regardless of do X thing or not.

707
00:59:44,820 --> 00:59:52,104
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that I feel a need to do anything to be at ease, in and of itself is an obligation of slavery.

708
00:59:52,164 --> 00:59:52,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

709
00:59:53,564 --> 00:59:53,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

710
00:59:57,543 --> 00:59:58,003
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the...

711
00:59:58,083 --> 01:00:02,569
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I spoke about it in... well, in a number of old talks.

712
01:00:02,909 --> 01:00:12,962
[SPEAKER_00]: I was referring to it as need to rest, like resting, which is exactly, I think, what you're referring to, the ease, which is, yeah, say, a presence of doubt,

713
01:00:13,743 --> 01:00:23,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, the soul... the primary, the fundamental, the root reason for engaging with it, for being hindered by it, is because you're trying to get rid of the discomfort of it.

714
01:00:23,772 --> 01:00:35,603
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why a person who is gripped by the hindrance of doubt would be revolving around and getting to the right answer, but then having the need to repeat it again and again and again, because it was never really about the answer.

715
01:00:35,643 --> 01:00:39,807
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about trying to find the ease which is measured by the absence of discomfort.

716
01:00:42,276 --> 01:00:45,958
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is pretty much a common thread to all the hindrances.

717
01:00:46,018 --> 01:00:46,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with sensuality.

718
01:00:46,879 --> 01:00:59,606
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said in other talks, it's inherently felt as unpleasant, which is why you seek the desire, the objects of your desire and acting out to cover that up and hope to replace it with pleasure and ease from it.

719
01:00:59,646 --> 01:01:00,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Same ill will.

720
01:01:00,987 --> 01:01:03,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if I don't say it, I'm staying with a discomfort.

721
01:01:03,608 --> 01:01:12,253
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to be eased from it, so I'll... maybe I won't do anything physically, maybe I won't say anything, but mentally I'll justify it, I'll revolve around it, I'll make policies like the example I gave.

722
01:01:12,333 --> 01:01:14,154
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, yeah.

723
01:01:14,854 --> 01:01:17,055
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that that's the only thing.

724
01:01:17,435 --> 01:01:21,578
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of... the hindrances are, as I said, they go as far

725
01:01:22,609 --> 01:01:33,553
[SPEAKER_00]: As to have the intention just for that mindless ease from the uncomfortable feeling, at the expense of perspective, that's also hindrance.

726
01:01:33,633 --> 01:01:37,295
[SPEAKER_00]: But acting out physically, verbally, stuff, that is also hindrance.

727
01:01:38,335 --> 01:01:43,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sometimes people identify just with these coarse things and think, oh, I'm free from hindrances because I'm not doing anything.

728
01:01:43,177 --> 01:01:47,639
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, are you free from the need for a gratuitous rest and ease?

729
01:01:48,739 --> 01:01:50,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Because making the right choice is effortless.

730
01:01:51,848 --> 01:02:06,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Needing to rest from doing it onto these things means giving way to the wild animal and allowing elephant to flinch and move and exercise some of its wild tendencies and preferences, because you can't be bothered.

731
01:02:09,115 --> 01:02:12,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, hindrances are pretty much, as I said, the scope of them

732
01:02:14,373 --> 01:02:28,484
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes from one end all the way to the subtlest kind of willing choices and acceptances of forms of lust, aversion, distraction and unwholesome states like doubt, restlessness and so on.

733
01:02:29,225 --> 01:02:30,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you have anxiety or worry.

734
01:02:30,826 --> 01:02:33,548
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't stop the mind from arising like that.

735
01:02:34,188 --> 01:02:35,109
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the mind of doubt.

736
01:02:35,189 --> 01:02:35,990
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't stop it.

737
01:02:36,831 --> 01:02:46,356
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can certainly never give in to the tendency to, oh, let me... let me do this or that, or give in to it a bit, so it's not as much.

738
01:02:47,516 --> 01:02:50,658
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, let me open a gate a bit and let it go around and then it'll come back.

739
01:02:51,719 --> 01:02:56,661
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, let me allow its wilderness, because I can't be bothered to train it fully to the necessary extent.

740
01:02:57,602 --> 01:02:59,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you still remain under the hindrances.

741
01:03:02,104 --> 01:03:04,145
[SPEAKER_00]: But, like with all the other aspects of training,

742
01:03:06,193 --> 01:03:07,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, things need to be addressed first.

743
01:03:08,014 --> 01:03:27,178
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're worrying about the aspects of rest, not you specifically, but just generally a person, right, I mustn't rest internally, context protect, but then still tolerate choices of physical, acting out of physical lust and verbal and anger and so on, it's like, yeah, like you have a bigger fish to fry first.

744
01:03:28,058 --> 01:03:32,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can see the scope and the subtlety and the depth of hindrances and address it.

745
01:03:33,921 --> 01:03:40,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, could you say it's the unquestioned mission of this needs to be solved that is the problem?

746
01:03:40,351 --> 01:03:41,753
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is not the phenomenon of doubt.

747
01:03:41,813 --> 01:03:42,634
[SPEAKER_00]: It will by itself.

748
01:03:42,814 --> 01:03:43,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

749
01:03:44,597 --> 01:03:46,139
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much what we answered now.

750
01:03:46,740 --> 01:03:47,341
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is

751
01:03:48,475 --> 01:04:01,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Like how the Sutta says, things like sign of beauty, sign of aversive, things suitable for doubt... Basically, the basis for hindrances are not what the hindrance is.

752
01:04:01,925 --> 01:04:06,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to deal with that means not basically failing to see that you're already hindered.

753
01:04:06,849 --> 01:04:07,529
[SPEAKER_00]: How are you hindered?

754
01:04:07,589 --> 01:04:10,251
[SPEAKER_00]: By wrongly attending to those things.

755
01:04:10,291 --> 01:04:10,671
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the

756
01:04:11,512 --> 01:04:17,337
[SPEAKER_00]: The most basic definition of wrongly attending to something is thinking that that's a problem when it actually isn't.

757
01:04:17,758 --> 01:04:20,300
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already a form of ayoniso manasikāra.

758
01:04:22,944 --> 01:04:31,010
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thinking to some extent, this deserves giving in, so to get the ease and resolution and fixing and so on.

759
01:04:31,930 --> 01:04:33,932
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, it's ayoniso manasikāra.

760
01:04:33,992 --> 01:04:40,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And as the Buddha said, it's the ayoniso manasikāra that makes things that are basis for hindrances, to be the hindrance.

761
01:04:40,677 --> 01:04:41,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Not those things.

762
01:04:42,118 --> 01:04:51,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the one who attends to them rightly, the sign of beauty remains, the sign of repulsive remains, things suitable for that... suitable to lend himself without, remain.

763
01:04:52,285 --> 01:04:53,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you're beyond hindrances.

764
01:04:55,728 --> 01:05:05,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I always go on about, you know, the whole kind of traditional common ideas, like, I must counter and deal with this or that, I must get rid of it, get rid of it, get rid of it, think the opposite, think the opposite.

765
01:05:05,916 --> 01:05:10,279
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yes, you're not seeing your mind there, you're just action-reaction, basically.

766
01:05:11,520 --> 01:05:14,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, thoughts come up, I do the opposite.

767
01:05:14,503 --> 01:05:14,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

768
01:05:15,483 --> 01:05:18,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of just blindly, I do the opposite, because Buddhaghosa said so.

769
01:05:19,687 --> 01:05:19,847
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

770
01:05:19,947 --> 01:05:21,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I doing the opposite?

771
01:05:22,074 --> 01:05:26,057
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, oh, because I absolutely hate the feeling of this.

772
01:05:26,077 --> 01:05:27,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because you're acting out of your will.

773
01:05:31,100 --> 01:05:32,501
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how is that going to free you from hindrance?

774
01:05:35,463 --> 01:05:35,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

775
01:05:37,165 --> 01:05:44,930
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why virtue, sensory strength, the gradual training before you can even come close to actually containing the hindrances rightly.

776
01:05:45,791 --> 01:05:45,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

777
01:05:46,011 --> 01:05:49,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when these thoughts present themselves, they will be pushing these choices.

778
01:05:50,025 --> 01:06:08,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yes, you don't welcome, entertain, choose, proliferate the thoughts of those choices, but you certainly don't try to, like, I must get rid of this state of citta altogether, because that would be rooted in pure aversion, pure resistance, which means that will hinder you.

779
01:06:09,098 --> 01:06:18,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you address every hindrance through your methods and managements, it will continue to hinder you, because the roots of your intentions remain unchanged.

780
01:06:21,967 --> 01:06:26,993
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and acting along with that attitude of this is a problem, keeps giving it validity, keep making it a problem.

781
01:06:27,273 --> 01:06:29,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Keeps making it a problem.

782
01:06:30,276 --> 01:06:30,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, yeah.

783
01:06:31,177 --> 01:06:37,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's why, as I said just now, learning is important, so that you can shift the emphasis of your views.

784
01:06:38,315 --> 01:06:43,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you need to learn that that's not a problem, despite your whole being feeling, no, that is a problem.

785
01:06:43,516 --> 01:06:50,457
[SPEAKER_00]: If only I say, do this, or react that, I will free myself from the confinement of this state of mind, of this problem.

786
01:06:50,557 --> 01:06:52,798
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have to go against that tendency, against that need.

787
01:06:54,138 --> 01:07:00,039
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only person who will do it, is the one that heard many times that, actually, no, that's not the problem.

788
01:07:00,119 --> 01:07:00,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Trust me on that.

789
01:07:00,800 --> 01:07:02,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Try doing it and you'll see it for yourself.

790
01:07:03,557 --> 01:07:07,759
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, literally, like a doctor telling you, stop eating that, it's harming you, but you don't see the connection.

791
01:07:08,219 --> 01:07:10,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't trust the doctor and you continue eating it.

792
01:07:11,280 --> 01:07:29,268
[SPEAKER_00]: But, for whatever, like, some person might actually, for one reason or the other, choose to, okay, I'll trust the doctor and, despite not having any problem with his food, not any issue with his food, I will stop eating it, because he said so, and maybe other symptoms will then show and I can see for myself that actually, yes, he was a problem.

793
01:07:29,328 --> 01:07:32,770
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a degree of faith is necessary, even when it comes to this.

